in shared with  virtual currency exchangers  and the proactive freezing of an account  of a payment processor for a value of  3.8 million dollars by financial  institution  we continue to work at collecting  relevant information on persons vehicles  and companies and remain in daily  communication with the financial  institution to assist them  information  it is  foreign  thanks for taking my questions stephen  hoff cc national news after it took you  almost three weeks to get rid of this  occupation  how do you go about restoring trucks  trust among residents and uh who expect  you to move quicker and how long do they  should they expect to see the  checkpoints and the increase increased  police presence downtown  so as i said in my in my comments  earlier on the building that community  trust and and entering into uh community  healing is an absolutely vital next step  for the ottawa police service and our  community partners uh this this unlawful  occupation of our streets has  dramatically impacted people in the  downtown core but i don't think there's  a resident of ottawa who hasn't felt the  hurt or pain that that these  demonstrators have caused to our city to  our community quite frankly to our  society  we  today as early as as late as today i've  been reaching out to community leaders  from across the city to start the  discussions about how do we start to  enter into those dialogues because they  are vitally important  we know as a police service we we have  public trust to gain back we we have  discussions dialogues learnings to do  from what happened and occurred up to  this weekend  as well as how we reform rebalance and  move forward those vitally important  community partnerships community  relations so as as the chief  and as representing every single member  of this police service we are deeply  committed to the community healing that  we know now needs to take place  um  hello  um thanks for taking our questions today  it's joining cbc news ottawa um  following on on the sort of you know how  long will the downtown be closed i see  that a lot of people are speculating  that maybe there's never going to be uh  traffic back on wellington street uh  security at parliament hill has often  spoken about that now that it's been  closed for three weeks  and i know it is the purview of  wellington street of the ottawa police  service  is there any sense that it might be  closed indefinitely  until we figure out you know how to  prevent this sort of occupation again i  notice that there are truckers there's  reports of the truckers having set up  down at vancleek hill so they're not  that far away  so one of the things that we identified  early on is we had a deliberate  well-resourced plans and commitment from  partners  i can tell you through discussions right  up to before we came down to this press  conference today there's a deep  commitment from our partners to continue  to support us  through the deputy commissioners that  i'm working here with today  through the support of commissioner  creek and commissioner lucky i can tell  you that the rcmp and opp will be  present with us till the end of this  operation and we're not done this  operation yet there is another phase  that will identify how we maintain the  streets how we eventually demobilize  once we've identified that there is no  threat of further uh protests coming to  our city we aren't there yet we will  over the next several days identify  what the posture of the police services  will look like to see how we maintain a  presence and make sure that nobody  returns to occupy our streets again  hi everyone and this is analyst at tvl  montreal can i ask my question in french  it's possible if you can ask your  english a question in english first i'll  make sure you get a response all right  no problem um  you said it's progressing very well uh  very well you say so when exactly and i  know it's difficult probably but you  must have a date a time for  a return to normal i think resident i  think people want to know that  information thank you  so i said from the beginning we we have  a plan and we're executing the plan the  one part of the plan that we don't  determine is the actual timelines of  when we complete it  we  began to execute our plan on thursday  afternoon  moved into an actions on phase on friday  morning and you've seen the results of  that over the weekend i still can't say  when  we will ultimately be completed this  operation because we now need to enter  into a maintenance portion of it to make  sure that what occurred three weeks ago  can never occur again so we will  continue to have a posture protect their  streets of our city protect our  community members from unlawful  occupations like occurred now moving  forward for as long as it takes  [Music]  good afternoon chief david menzies with  rebel news um chief can you kindly  explain how it was that my colleague to  my right here alexa lavoie was shot  point blank with a tear gas canister  doing some very painful damage to her  what was the reason for that given that  she was simply  practicing journalism in the public  square  uh so i'm unfamiliar with the incident  you're speaking of what i can tell you  is that there is complaint mechanisms  there is review mechanisms that will be  engaged following this for any use of  force incident that occurred  i'll also say that it's been my  observations and experience through the  amazing extensive journalism coverage  that's occurred through this that the  vast majority of our members have been  extremely professional they have  executed an extremely methodical plan  that has been focused on the safety of  the residents the safety of our officers  and the safety of the people engaged in  the protest and chief just one follow-up  question if i may um  given that the emergency  act requires certain benchmarks to be  met such as the safety of canadians must  be at peril  the sovereignty of the nation must be at  peril  do you think those benchmarks were met  given this was for what i could tell a  largely  peaceful demonstration and protest so i  guess i'm asking did you really need to  have this act and vote in terms of uh  what were minor unlawful uh acts and uh  basically bylaw infractions  so what the the federal government the  provincial government both uh enacted  legislation that was absolutely  supportive to helping us to end the  illegal occupation of our streets uh  what could be described as bylaw  infractions were actually terrorizing  moments for the city for the city the  residents and the communities that came  around those areas  the powers that were provided to us  through the emergencies act through the  provincial emergency act through the  injunction and through any of the other  legislative meetings that we got to end  this protest were extremely beneficial  in us being able to be where we are  today see clear streets and have  residents that can again walk through  their downtown core that they own thank  you  hi there eric ibrahim the canadian press  if  my recollection is correct  it was mentioned that  191  people were arrested at 107 are charged  i'm wondering if you can tell us about  the remaining  number in custody and and what we might  be able to expect as far as charges laid  with them  so what i can tell you is that we will  on a regular basis update our  investigative progress uh you will see  more people there will be updated uh  numbers in terms of arrests that have  occurred as a result of  unlawful protests and demonstrations  today  you will see series of charges coming  out after that and we will continue to  update on our investigations and charges  on an ongoing basis  okay thank you and just one quick  follow-up um i i have heard a report  that um police were giving um  uh perhaps a piece of paper or something  of that nature to truckers or  demonstrators as they left the perimeter  i was wondering if you could descri